+# Use Redis for disposable catalog results
+
+For whoever changes the catalog or operates larder. Redis may cache item searches because the item page is slow on
+Saturday mornings and Tom can run Redis on the server; Postgres remains the source of truth.
+
+## Context
+
+The item page is slow on Saturday mornings. The requested remedy is a Redis cache, with Tom available to run the
+service. The repository has no query timings or load measurements establishing the cause. Previous decisions kept
+[sessions in Postgres](0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[jobs in Postgres](0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md), after Redis evicted receipt jobs and only one volunteer
+knew how to restore it. A catalog cache must tolerate eviction and outages, and bound stale stock and request delays.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Keep querying Postgres**: keeps the service count down and stock current, but provides no reuse for repeated
+  searches. Query profiling and database tuning remain possible follow-up work if caching is insufficient.
+- **Cache in each web process**: adds no service, but duplicates results and misses across web processes.
+- **Redis for disposable catalog results**: chosen as requested, with Tom providing server operation; shared results
+  can be lost without losing pantry orders, sessions or jobs.
+
+## Decision
+
+Use Redis only as an optional cache of catalog search results. Its shared keys allow repeated searches across web
+processes to reuse results. Cache failure falls back to Postgres, and a short expiry bounds stock staleness. The
+[catalog feature](../features/catalog.md) describes setup and limits.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Repeated searches within the expiry can avoid the item query. Redis adds a service to operate, and Tom's availability
+alone does not establish broader support coverage. Eviction or an outage restores the database load; a cold cache
+does not address a slow underlying query. Sessions and jobs retain their existing stores.
